.TH CLOG_SET_SIGNAL_POLICY 3 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_signal_policy, clog_signal_policy_t \- choose what the logger may do about SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

typedef enum {
    CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO      = 0,   /* the default */
    CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED = 1,
} clog_signal_policy_t;

ccol_retval_t clog_set_signal_policy(clog_signal_policy_t policy);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets, for the whole process, what the library may do about the two
signals that a logger's write can raise: SIGPIPE, when a logger writes
to a descriptor whose reader is gone, and SIGXFSZ, when it writes to a
regular file that has reached the file size limit of the process.  With
the default disposition, either signal ends the process.

A write(2) to a pipe or a socket whose reading end is closed fails
with EPIPE and also raises SIGPIPE.  A write(2) to a regular file that
would pass the RLIMIT_FSIZE limit of the process (which ulimit -f, or
the LimitFSIZE= setting of a systemd unit, sets) writes only what fits
and raises SIGXFSZ, and every later write to the file, including the
newline and the marker with which the logger repairs it, fails with
EFBIG and raises SIGXFSZ again.  A logger finds out the kind of its
descriptor once, with fstat(2), when it opens (a rotation keeps a
regular file), and what happens depends on that kind:

Socket
    Under either policy the library writes with send(2) and
    MSG_NOSIGNAL, so the write fails with EPIPE and raises nothing.
    No signal state changes.

Pipe or FIFO, under CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO
    The async writer thread of a logger keeps SIGPIPE blocked in its
    own signal mask and takes back the SIGPIPE that its own failed
    write left pending on it, so nothing about the process changes.

    macOS raises the SIGPIPE of a pipe write on the whole process
    rather than on the writing thread, so there the async writer thread
    also sets F_SETNOSIGPIPE on the descriptor for the length of each
    write, and the write fails with EPIPE and raises nothing.  After
    the write the descriptor gets back the F_SETNOSIGPIPE value it had
    before.

    Any other thread that writes to a pipe sets the disposition of
    SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN for the whole process the first time it does
    so.  That write comes from a synchronous logger, from a CLOG_FATAL
    record, or from a record that an async logger writes on the
    calling thread because its queue refused it.  Opening a logger on a
    pipe changes nothing; only the first such write does.

Regular file, under CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO
    Opening the logger, and each rotation that opens the next file,
    sets the disposition of SIGXFSZ to SIG_IGN for the whole process.
    It happens at the open and not at the first write that meets the
    limit, because after a record that ends exactly at the limit the
    next write raises SIGXFSZ with no short write before it.
    A write at the limit then fails with EFBIG, and the logger repairs
    the file once it has room, as clog_create_from_file_mp(3)
    describes.  The async writer thread of the logger and the thread
    that compresses its rotated files also keep SIGXFSZ blocked in
    their own signal masks, and take back the SIGXFSZ that their own
    failed write left pending on them.

Pipe, FIFO or regular file, under CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED
    The library changes no disposition and no signal mask.  The write
    raises SIGPIPE or SIGXFSZ, and what happens is up to the
    disposition and the signal mask of the application.  An application
    that writes logs under a file size limit must then ignore SIGXFSZ,
    or block it in every thread that can write a log file; otherwise
    the kernel ends the process once a log file reaches the limit.

Terminal, or any other descriptor
    The write can raise neither signal, so the library does nothing
    about it under either policy.

The library changes the disposition of each signal at most once, and
only when the disposition is SIG_DFL at that moment, so a handler or
an ignore that the application installed stays in place.

Whatever the policy, a record whose write fails with EPIPE is dropped
silently, the same as a record that meets any other write error the
library cannot recover from.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
policy  CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO or CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       The policy is in force for every later write and
                   open.
ccol_invalid_args  policy is not one of the two values above.
                   Nothing changes.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You may call this at any time, from any thread, before or after any
logger exists.  The policy governs every write and every open that
starts after the call, including those of the async writer threads
that already run: under CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED such a thread unblocks
SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ again before its next write, and under
CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO it blocks them again.  A thread that its creator
started with one of the two signals already blocked keeps it blocked
under both policies.

A disposition of SIG_IGN that the library already set under
CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO stays in place when the policy changes to
CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED, because the library cannot tell whether that
disposition is still its own, and the application may already rely on
it.  To keep both dispositions untouched from the start, call this
before the first logger opens.

Linux raises the SIGXFSZ of a write on the thread that wrote, but
FreeBSD raises it on the whole process, where any thread that does not
block it can take it.  Under CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED, blocking SIGXFSZ
only in the threads that log is therefore enough on Linux and not on
FreeBSD, while ignoring it protects the process on both.

A disposition of SIG_IGN holds for the whole process, and, as for any
ignored signal, a child that fork(2) and exec(3) start inherits it.
An application whose children expect the default disposition of
SIGPIPE or SIGXFSZ either restores it in the child before exec(3), or
picks CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED and handles both signals itself.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Thread-safe.  A write or an open that runs at the same time as the
call follows either the old policy or the new one.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* The application handles SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ itself: it wants no
 * change to their dispositions, so it ignores SIGXFSZ and blocks
 * SIGPIPE in the threads that log. */
if (clog_set_signal_policy(CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED) != ccol_success)
    abort();
signal(SIGXFSZ, SIG_IGN);
clog lg = clog_create_from_fd(STDOUT_FILENO, CLOG_INFO, NULL);
if (lg == CLOG_INVALID)
    abort();
clog_info(lg, "stdout may be a pipe");
clog_close(lg);
.fi
